WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
THE TRUTH ABOUT GOODWILL:
Goodwill? More like Illwill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.820
At first glance, Goodwill seems like the friendly&nbsp;
neighborhood thrift store focused on giving back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:14.820
to the community and fighting inequality.&nbsp;
On paper, they’re a non profit that isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.880
owned by anyone which means no shareholders, no&nbsp;
stock, and hopefully no anticonsumer behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.340
Their board of directors is actually filled&nbsp;
with a bunch of volunteers, so theoretically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:32.700
they’re only goal would be to give back to the&nbsp;
community which they do in several ways. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.360
have a bunch of career programs that help&nbsp;
underprivileged people make some cash and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.340
learn new skills. They’re also one of the only&nbsp;
organizations out there that will hire people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.560
with disabilites aside from the government.&nbsp;
They’ve also been around for quite some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.560
time as Goodwill has been around for over 120&nbsp;
years which means that they’ve donated tens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:59.280
billions if not over a hundred billion dollars to&nbsp;
charitable services. In fact, in 2018 alone, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.220
pulled in $6.1 billion in revenue out of which&nbsp;
$5.27 billion was spent on charitable services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:14.520
So, they’re doing a great job right? Well, yes and&nbsp;
no. There are definitely commendable portions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.520
Goodwill and I don’t want to take away from that.&nbsp;
But, there’s also a lot of reprehensible portions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:27.120
as well. For one, Goodwill isn’t even all that&nbsp;
charitable in the first place. Goodwill claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.820
that 82% of their revenue goes towards programs&nbsp;
and services for people in need. Some regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:40.260
even have percentages as high as 90%. But, this&nbsp;
is largely misleading as this does not mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:47.640
82% or 90% of the money that you spend at Goodwill&nbsp;
goes the charity. In fact, there’s evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:53.700
as little as 1/8th of their profit actually goes&nbsp;
to the main programs that they advertise. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.760
you know how Goodwill employs disabled people?&nbsp;
Well, this may be more exploitative than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.820
generous. You see, there’s special laws that allow&nbsp;
organizations to pay disabled people as little as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:13.800
22 to 41 cents per hour which is already quite bad&nbsp;
but it doesn’t stop there. Disabled employees also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.840
don’t have the same protections that everyone else&nbsp;
does which makes them the perfect scapegoat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.700
hand off dangerous tasks to. And when it comes&nbsp;
to whistleblowers and employees fighting back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:30.420
Goodwill may as well just be a private corporation&nbsp;
as that’s how they behave. They usually threaten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.880
fire, and publicly discredit people&nbsp;
that fight back. So, here’s the truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.340
about Goodwill and why you should think twice&nbsp;
before donating to or purchasing from Goodwill.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:48.840
QUESTIONABLE DONATIONS:
Jumping straight into their donations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.640
Goodwill claims that they spend between 80 and&nbsp;
90% of their revenue on charitable services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:59.760
and technically speaking this is probably&nbsp;
accurate. As we touched on, in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.960
Goodwill pulled in $6.1 billion and donated&nbsp;
$5.27 billion. So, that falls right in line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:13.260
with about 86% of their revenue being donated.&nbsp;
But this doesn’t mean what you think it means.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.900
Any normal person would probably think that this&nbsp;
means that for every dollar you spend at Goodwill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:25.560
86 cents gets donated. Or atleast, maybe for&nbsp;
every dollar they profit, they donate 86 cents but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:31.380
neither of these are true. You see, the reality is&nbsp;
that much of Goodwill’s charitable spending isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.420
even funded by us the customers, it’s actually&nbsp;
funded by various government organizations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.240
Take Goodwill Omaha for instance. They have a&nbsp;
bunch of job training and assistance programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.540
but nearly all of these activities were funded&nbsp;
by government grants and contracts. And you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:54.120
their program to employ disabled people? Well,&nbsp;
that was primarily funded by school districts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.860
It turns out that out of the $4 million&nbsp;
worth of profit generated by the region, only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.940
$557,000 or a little more than an eighth was spent&nbsp;
on these flagship job programs. And keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:13.200
that’s profit, not revenue. Only 1/8th of the&nbsp;
profit is being spent on these programs, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.140
imagine how little of the consumer based revenue&nbsp;
is being spent on these programs. But, then where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:24.900
are all of these profits going instead? People&nbsp;
are literally donating items for free, so surely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.700
Goodwill shouldn’t have too many manufacturing&nbsp;
or shipping or sourcing expenses. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:36.120
the official reasoning is administrative overhead,&nbsp;
but that’s just the fancy term for large executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:42.300
compensation. Goodwill actually has a history&nbsp;
of paying executives super large salaries. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:50.820
2014 for example, they paid their CEO Frank McGree&nbsp;
$933,000. This actually won him so much criticism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.680
and backlash that he would end up resigning.&nbsp;
But, things haven’t exactly gotten better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:02.880
His successors were paid a base salary of $434,000&nbsp;
and there are rumors that total comp reached as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:10.080
high as $1.1 million, though Goodwill disputes&nbsp;
the total compensation numbers. Either way though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:16.260
executives and high level managers are being paid&nbsp;
multiple six figures. In fact, Change.org claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:22.440
that the top managers at a 150 Goodwill locations&nbsp;
are being paid more than $30 million. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.640
some of these people don’t seem to have any&nbsp;
issue charging a bunch of personal expenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:33.420
onto corporate cards. Former CFO Tim Ligon, for&nbsp;
example, charged over $75,000 worth of personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:39.840
purchases onto a Goodwill corporate card. So,&nbsp;
yeah, that’s where much of the profit is going.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.900
Also, for the record, I have no issue with people&nbsp;
making a lot of money. If you wanna make a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:52.380
money, go to a private company or start your own&nbsp;
business. Don’t leach off of a non profit. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.500
one more thing about these executives is that many&nbsp;
of them are politicans by trade. The current CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.000
Steve Preston, for example, was the secretary&nbsp;
of housing and urban development under President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.520
George Bush. And the former CEO, Jim Gibbons was&nbsp;
part of the White House Council for Community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.820
Solutions under President Barak Obama, so&nbsp;
take that for what you will. But, it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:20.280
just your donations that Goodwill is exploiting.&nbsp;
They’re also exploiting the people who work there.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:26.280
EXPLOITING THE DISABLED:&nbsp;

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.620
One of the trademarks of Goodwill is providing&nbsp;
jobs to underprivileged people and especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:38.400
people with disabilites. But, once again, this&nbsp;
isn’t as great as it sounds. It’s actually really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.460
bad. You see, Goodwill leverages an archaic rule&nbsp;
found in the Fair Labor Standards Act which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:51.240
passed way back in 1938. Section 14c of the act&nbsp;
basically allows companies to legally pay disabled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:56.400
workers wages that are lower than minimum&nbsp;
wage. This has resulted in disabled people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:03.000
in Pennsylvania earning as little as 22 cents,&nbsp;
38 cents, and 41 cents per hour. Goodwill claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.820
that these are outlier cases and they’re probably&nbsp;
right about that. But, just because the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:13.980
disabled worker isn’t making these absurdly&nbsp;
low wages doesn’t mean that they’re making good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:19.620
wages either. Also, I wanted to point out that&nbsp;
it’s not just Goodwill that’s exploiting these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:24.360
rules either. Basically every organization that&nbsp;
hires disabled workers other than the govenrment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:29.820
leverage this tactic. Disabled workers at&nbsp;
Applebee’s for example were making $3.97 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:35.460
$5.96 per hour. And disabled workers at Barnes&nbsp;
&amp; Noble were making $3.80 to $4.85 per hour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:41.100
So, it’s kind of an industry wide problem and&nbsp;
it doesn’t seem like it will fixed anytime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:47.280
soon either. Goodwill has actaully fought against&nbsp;
proposals that suggest raising the minimum wage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:53.280
Their reasoning is that they simply can’t&nbsp;
afford to pay employees anymore which doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.820
really make any sense when you consider how&nbsp;
much their executives make and how little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:02.160
actually gets donated. But, not only does&nbsp;
Goodwill pay disabled workers low wages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.920
they also expect a lot of work. In fact,&nbsp;
disabled workers are straight up tested for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.840
effectiveness. Disabled employees have to take a&nbsp;
timed test to see how quickly they can sort and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:19.380
hang clothes. The ideal rate is 100 garments&nbsp;
within 32 minutes. For most people, this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:25.740
pretty reasonable target. But, if you’re blind for&nbsp;
example, this becomes exponentially harder. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:31.680
if they don’t reach this target, your hourly wage&nbsp;
gets deducted proportionately. This is how you end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.440
up with employees making ridiculously low wages.&nbsp;
One blind Goodwill employee named Sheila Leigland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:44.280
for example, was already only making $3.99 per&nbsp;
hour. But, Goodwill wanted to reduce this to $2.75&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:50.880
an hour. Sheila would end up quitting but this is&nbsp;
not a luxury that most disabled workers have. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:56.280
they’re just stuck making $2.75 an hour. Goodwill&nbsp;
claims that they’re not exploiting workers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.080
that they’re actually providing opportunity&nbsp;
as 80% of disabled people in the US don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:06.840
any opportunity at all. But pity jobs that milk&nbsp;
these people for the lowest wages possible isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:11.400
the same as truly uplifting and supporting&nbsp;
the disabled community. As Alice put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:16.140
at least we gave them jobs is not a&nbsp;
progressive, socially responsible move.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.240
WORKPLACE DEATHS:&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:28.020
If you didn’t think that exploiting disabled&nbsp;
workers was bad enough, well, it gets even worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:33.960
as Goodwill has been linked with several workplace&nbsp;
deaths. Goodwill’s PR and legal team love to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:38.820
off these deaths as tragic incidents that were&nbsp;
not their fault. But, when you take a look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:44.040
the evidence, that story becomes a lot harder&nbsp;
to believe. Take former Goodwill employee Dave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.440
Goudie for example. Dave only worked at Goodwill&nbsp;
for a mere 6 months as a waste management driver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:55.320
but let’s just say that he went to hell and back&nbsp;
during his short tenure. One day during his shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.320 --> 00:10:00.840
he noticed that the trailer brakes weren’t working&nbsp;
when activated from the cab. So, as a responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:06.240
driver, he would pull over, shut down the truck,&nbsp;
and call management. He was hoping that Goodwill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:11.340
would sent out a technician who could fix the&nbsp;
issue. But, instead, they just sent out another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:17.580
driver that was willing to drive the truck despite&nbsp;
the issue. This was no doubt jarring for Dave and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:22.860
he would of course go ahead and file complaints to&nbsp;
Goodwill regarding the incident. But, this was by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:27.960
no means the only safety hazard that he noticed.&nbsp;
He also noticed a major safety hazard regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:33.360
Goodwill’s trash compactors. He reported quote&nbsp;
“Based on my personal observations, most employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.580
now operating compactors at each plant have not&nbsp;
received the required training.” He would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:44.400
warn that this could result in injuries, deaths,&nbsp;
and fines which could be completely avoided if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:50.100
proper training was implemented. But, as fate&nbsp;
would have it, Goodwill would take no action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:55.800
and a 26 year old employee named Abraham Garza&nbsp;
would be killed trash compactor just a few weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:00.900
later. This would’ve been a good time for Goodwill&nbsp;
to apologize to Dave for not taking his complaints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:08.640
more seriously but instead, they would fire Dave&nbsp;
and blame the incident on Abraham. Cal OSHA would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:15.060
eventually step in and fine Goodwill $106,000,&nbsp;
but even after that, Goodwill would continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:20.460
maintain that they were not liable. If this&nbsp;
was a one off incident, they would have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:26.880
plausible deniability but Goodwill actually&nbsp;
has a history of onsite injuries. In 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:33.120
a 27 year old was crushed to death by a trash&nbsp;
compactor lift. In 2014, a 69 year old was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:40.740
crushed by a forklift. And in 2017, a 54 year old&nbsp;
was killed by another forklift. And the worst part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:46.560
is that many of these deaths are amongst disabled&nbsp;
workers. You see, employers aren’t as liable for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:52.140
disabled workers because the disability may&nbsp;
increase the chances of a workplace injury. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:57.960
Goodwill is literally incentivized to place&nbsp;
disabled workers in dangerous jobs and it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:03.300
like that is exactly what they’re doing leading&nbsp;
to many disabled workers literally being killed.

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:05.580
IllWILL:&nbsp;

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:16.380
Those are just a few of the most notable issues at&nbsp;
Goodwill but they’re by no means the only issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:21.660
For example, another major critic against Goodwill&nbsp;
is that they send clothes that they can’t sell in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:26.520
western countries to developing countries.&nbsp;
And given that these were donated items,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:32.400
they can sell these clothes for super cheap which&nbsp;
screws up the local clothing industries. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:38.580
only reason that Goodwill is able to get away with&nbsp;
so much is because they’re a non profit. If a for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:43.560
profit company like Amazon was exploiting disabled&nbsp;
workers by paying them ridiculously low wages and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:48.960
exposing them to significant workplace risk,&nbsp;
they would be crucified by public opinion. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:55.260
since Goodwill is a non profit/charity, these&nbsp;
issues often get glossed over. Now again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.260 --> 00:13:01.920
this isn’t to say that Goodwill is completely evil&nbsp;
like big pharma or something. They do some awesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:07.620
things but they’ve also got some major issues&nbsp;
that they have been ignoring for way too long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:13.260
And just maybe, if we bring enough attention&nbsp;
to these issues, we can actually get them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:20.820
change but that’s just what I think. Do you think&nbsp;
Goodwill is really good will or ill will? Comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

